WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.080
Film Courage: What's the best part of the
writing process for you?

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.100
Zach Zerries, Screenwriter/Author: That is
a a profoundly good question.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:20.470
I think when you find that synergy between
your characters and the plot and again things

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:28.840
are starting to roll and and it could be in
the outline phase too I've had plenty of experiences

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:35.390
with you know having that breakthrough where
you're like oh right this is this is where

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:43.140
I need to go it's not I don't think it's necessarily
one thing it could be you know you write it

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:49.110
you write a terrific scene or a great chapter
and you're just like yeah then of course you

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.300
know you could always go back the next day
and think what the hell did I write but that

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.920
doesn't happen as much I think when you feel
when you feel like you really pulled out and

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.260
you really expressed yourself in a way that
or had the characters express themselves in

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:12.350
a way that you know resonates with you I again
I think I think it it's it can be any it's

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:20.509
it can be anything again from you know really
thinking up a cool plot point to really getting

00:01:20.509 --> 00:01:25.990
that lock that log line down we were like
oh this is you know I'm on with this and I'm

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:31.539
one of those people who's I know William Broyles
Jr. who is a terrific screenwriter he's one

00:01:31.539 --> 00:01:35.579
of the best you know he always I remember
reading an interview with him years ago saying

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:45.170
never be satisfied and I agree I I'm not always
satisfied I always want to I always think

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:48.610
I can do things better I'm a perfectionist
I always want to continue to mine and if somebody

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:54.279
says to me this we can do that I'm like great
let's let's do it let's mine this let's let's

00:01:54.279 --> 00:02:06.649
keep let's dig so so for me I think when you
know the the the the time where I really feel

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:11.390
where I love with the process that I love
is I think it's just it's the discovery discovery

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:19.220
of again your character where they're going
and it could be you know the characters and

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.549
is in trouble and how do we get them or her
out of it what's a creative way of doing it

00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:28.989
and always thinking you know again I hate
the expression outside the box but thinking

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:35.920
outside the box coming with a new way and
a new twist and and how that you know being

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.040
inventive and when that works and it's working
on the page and you or you think it's working

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:47.349
on the page and you're like oh that's cool
and it opens up and it's that it's those those

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:54.900
psychological breakthroughs in a sense in
in regard to the creative process so when

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.709
you start to see that wow these dots are connecting
this is actually working it's great it's great

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:09.740
it really is it's yeah it gives you it it
gives you a you know confidence and you you

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.689
just feel it you know it's it buoys your spirit
a little bit like I said the next day you

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:19.340
could go back and you're like okay I got through
this now you know that's why I get it's good

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.740
to have an outline but even in having the
outline and knowing you know how do you start

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:26.620
up especially in you know writing you know
writing that next scene or writing the next

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.739
chapter you're still staring at a blank page
you have an idea of where you want to go but

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:36.329
then it's a matter of you know really inventing
that and it's that's something else that's

00:03:36.329 --> 00:03:41.819
exhilarating but also daunting at the same
time without a doubt what's the fastest you've

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:50.260
ever written a full screenplay and did that
screenplay get made fastest I actually I wrote

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:56.500
take back pretty quickly the first draft was
probably a couple of weeks after it outlined

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:03.510
it it was pretty quick it took a little while
to get made and it went through subsequently

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:09.799
